{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Het8nAwPLRUdAhnWDaDhKU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — exec-sim-repair-verifier\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25, 2026)\n5\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Extended fallback: ICSE 2027 (Jun 23 abstract, Jun 30 paper)\n7\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (14 days from project start)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Claims (3 conditions)\n10\t\n11\t1. **Task Reformulation**: Binary pass/fail classification from (patched_code, test_code) pairs is a simpler and more accurate task than execution trace/output generation (CWM-style), achieving higher verification accuracy with the same training data and model size. This reformulation sidesteps two dominant CWM failure modes: token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction errors (Debugging CWMs, arXiv:2602.07672).\n12\t\n13\t2. **Diagnostic Signals**: Per-test pass/fail predictions provide actionable diagnostic information (which specific tests a patch fails) that enables targeted re-repair, beyond what trajectory-level binary resolved/unresolved scoring offers. (Contingent on P1 downstream validation: if diagnostic-rerepair experiment shows no significant resolve rate improvement, this claim downgrades to \"hypothesis\" and paper focuses on Claims 1+3.)\n14\t\n15\t3. **Accuracy-Utility Frontier Characterization**: The effectiveness of per-test verification depends critically on test distribution characteristics. We empirically show: (a) per-test advantage concentrates in instances with multiple FAIL_TO_PASS tests (31% of SWE-bench Verified); (b) PASS_TO_PASS regression detection provides signal only when regression rate exceeds ~10%; (c) block-correlated prediction errors amplify per-test advantage through reduced effective degrees of freedom. These findings provide a practical decision framework for when per-test simulation is worth deploying.\n16\t\n17\t## Story Spine\n18\t\n19\t**Problem**: Test-time scaling for code repair requires ranking candidate patches, but verification paradigms face a granularity-cost tradeoff. Full execution costs ~$1-3 and minutes per candidate. Execution-free alternatives like SWE-RM predict trajectory-level binary correctness at low cost but provide only coarse signals that cannot distinguish between candidates failing on different test subsets.\n20\t\n21\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Code World Models (Self-Execution Simulation, CWM) predict execution outputs but suffer from token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction failures. SWE-RM achieves strong trajectory-level scoring but loses per-test diagnostic information. No work studies per-test binary classification as a dedicated verification formulation, or compares classification vs generation formulations for code execution simulation at controlled model sizes.\n22\t\n23\t**Our framing**: We reformulate execution simulation from an open-ended generation task (predict execution trace/output) to a closed-form classification task (predict binary pass/fail per test). This reduces the output space from unbounded token sequences to a single binary decision, removing the two dominant failure modes of Code World Models. We empirically ground this reformulation through (1) an oracle accuracy-utility frontier analysis revealing when per-test granularity helps, (2) training experiments comparing classification vs generation at matched model size and data, and (3) downstream repair experiments showing diagnostic signal translates to practical improvement.\n24\t\n25\t**Headline result template**: \"On function-level benchmarks, a 4B classification verifier achieves [X]% per-class F1 while a matched generation model achieves [Y]%, demonstrating that reformulating execution simulation as classification improves verification accuracy by [Z] percentage points. On repo-level repair, per-test diagnostic signals improve targeted re-repair resolve rate by [W]%.\"\n26\t\n27\t## Venue Fit Risks\n28\t\n29\t1. **EMNLP vs SE venue**: Core concern is code repair verification, which may be perceived as SE rather than NLP. Mitigation: frame as (a) probing LLM code semantic reasoning capabilities through per-test prediction, and (b) a general NLP methodology insight about classification vs generation formulations applicable beyond code.\n30\t\n31\t2. **Negative P2P finding**: P2P regression rate ~1% means per-test is not universally better. Must present honestly as a characterization finding, not bury it. This actually strengthens the paper by showing nuanced understanding rather than overclaiming.\n32\t\n33\t3. **Function-level to repo-level gap**: MVP validated on HumanEval/MBPP (function-level). Repo-level extension requires SWE-bench Docker infrastructure and is resource-intensive. If repo-level results are weak, paper scope may need to contract to function-level + oracle analysis.\n34\t\n35\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Mitigations\n36\t\n37\t| Concern | Mitigation Experiment |\n38\t|---------|----------------------|\n39\t| \"Classification is trivially easier than generation -- not interesting\" | Trace-then-Judge ablation isolates classification vs generation contribution |\n40\t| \"Per-test is just trajectory-level with extra steps when T=1\" | Oracle frontier analysis shows T distribution sensitivity; honest reporting of T=1 equivalence |\n41\t| \"No practical downstream impact\" | Diagnostic re-repair experiment shows resolve rate improvement |\n42\t| \"Small-scale function-level results don't generalize\" | Repo-level SWE-bench extension (if resources allow) |\n43\t| \"CWM comparison is unfair (different output format)\" | Matched model, data, compute; discuss target asymmetry limitation |\n44\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e2ff9bf-0df6-4a54-a6c1-9e43e44e7de7","uuid":"8b481ea3-468f-4752-a9a4-30211fa381d6","timestamp":"2026-05-11T21:54:08.457Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — exec-sim-repair-verifier\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25, 2026)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Extended fallback: ICSE 2027 (Jun 23 abstract, Jun 30 paper)\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (14 days from project start)\n\n## Core Claims (3 conditions)\n\n1. **Task Reformulation**: Binary pass/fail classification from (patched_code, test_code) pairs is a simpler and more accurate task than execution trace/output generation (CWM-style), achieving higher verification accuracy with the same training data and model size. This reformulation sidesteps two dominant CWM failure modes: token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction errors (Debugging CWMs, arXiv:2602.07672).\n\n2. **Diagnostic Signals**: Per-test pass/fail predictions provide actionable diagnostic information (which specific tests a patch fails) that enables targeted re-repair, beyond what trajectory-level binary resolved/unresolved scoring offers. (Contingent on P1 downstream validation: if diagnostic-rerepair experiment shows no significant resolve rate improvement, this claim downgrades to \"hypothesis\" and paper focuses on Claims 1+3.)\n\n3. **Accuracy-Utility Frontier Characterization**: The effectiveness of per-test verification depends critically on test distribution characteristics. We empirically show: (a) per-test advantage concentrates in instances with multiple FAIL_TO_PASS tests (31% of SWE-bench Verified); (b) PASS_TO_PASS regression detection provides signal only when regression rate exceeds ~10%; (c) block-correlated prediction errors amplify per-test advantage through reduced effective degrees of freedom. These findings provide a practical decision framework for when per-test simulation is worth deploying.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Test-time scaling for code repair requires ranking candidate patches, but verification paradigms face a granularity-cost tradeoff. Full execution costs ~$1-3 and minutes per candidate. Execution-free alternatives like SWE-RM predict trajectory-level binary correctness at low cost but provide only coarse signals that cannot distinguish between candidates failing on different test subsets.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Code World Models (Self-Execution Simulation, CWM) predict execution outputs but suffer from token-budget exhaustion and string-value prediction failures. SWE-RM achieves strong trajectory-level scoring but loses per-test diagnostic information. No work studies per-test binary classification as a dedicated verification formulation, or compares classification vs generation formulations for code execution simulation at controlled model sizes.\n\n**Our framing**: We reformulate execution simulation from an open-ended generation task (predict execution trace/output) to a closed-form classification task (predict binary pass/fail per test). This reduces the output space from unbounded token sequences to a single binary decision, removing the two dominant failure modes of Code World Models. We empirically ground this reformulation through (1) an oracle accuracy-utility frontier analysis revealing when per-test granularity helps, (2) training experiments comparing classification vs generation at matched model size and data, and (3) downstream repair experiments showing diagnostic signal translates to practical improvement.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"On function-level benchmarks, a 4B classification verifier achieves [X]% per-class F1 while a matched generation model achieves [Y]%, demonstrating that reformulating execution simulation as classification improves verification accuracy by [Z] percentage points. On repo-level repair, per-test diagnostic signals improve targeted re-repair resolve rate by [W]%.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n1. **EMNLP vs SE venue**: Core concern is code repair verification, which may be perceived as SE rather than NLP. Mitigation: frame as (a) probing LLM code semantic reasoning capabilities through per-test prediction, and (b) a general NLP methodology insight about classification vs generation formulations applicable beyond code.\n\n2. **Negative P2P finding**: P2P regression rate ~1% means per-test is not universally better. Must present honestly as a characterization finding, not bury it. This actually strengthens the paper by showing nuanced understanding rather than overclaiming.\n\n3. **Function-level to repo-level gap**: MVP validated on HumanEval/MBPP (function-level). Repo-level extension requires SWE-bench Docker infrastructure and is resource-intensive. If repo-level results are weak, paper scope may need to contract to function-level + oracle analysis.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Mitigations\n\n| Concern | Mitigation Experiment |\n|---------|----------------------|\n| \"Classification is trivially easier than generation -- not interesting\" | Trace-then-Judge ablation isolates classification vs generation contribution |\n| \"Per-test is just trajectory-level with extra steps when T=1\" | Oracle frontier analysis shows T distribution sensitivity; honest reporting of T=1 equivalence |\n| \"No practical downstream impact\" | Diagnostic re-repair experiment shows resolve rate improvement |\n| \"Small-scale function-level results don't generalize\" | Repo-level SWE-bench extension (if resources allow) |\n| \"CWM comparison is unfair (different output format)\" | Matched model, data, compute; discuss target asymmetry limitation |\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}}}
